 work exactly as  it would on the original image so that's  something that we were able to do before  here's something that's new so we have  this facility called ICANN eyes we're  gonna go ahead and evaluate this right  now because this takes just a little bit  to evaluate what I can eyes allows us to  do is it allows us to take what may be a  large and messy thing that lives in the  kernel and embed it in your notebook so  in this case well I might have a problem  here partly because this did take a long  time to evaluate partly because github  has some limitations where if you hit  them too hard too many times from a  given machine then they may block you  out for an hour  or something like that I don't want that  to happen so what I've done is I've  created an ionized blob for lack of a  better word here that represents the  entire data and so now I can actually  begin just doing just treating it as if  it were the in place data and so I'm  just going to assign it to a variable  show some outputs now the important part  of this to understand is that this data  actually lives in the notebook so I  could delete this cell entirely and  leave only this cell and then give it to  someone else and they'll be able to work  with the same data set that I'm using  here so in this case I've made an  ionized blob by using the icon eyes  command you can also make an ionized  blob let me collapse this you can also  make an ionized blob interactively and  so I'm just going to take let's say the  first 25 elements of that data and here  well it's kind of messy maybe I want to  embed this as an example but I don't  want to look at this this is kind of  silly I maybe I want to get the flavor  of it but I want to look at it well  let's use our triple click drag trick  which we learned and I'm going to triple  click drag oh let's say just everything  is set the first now in the last element  now I'm going to right click and I'm  going to choose I can eyes and now this  is an equivalent expression if we  evaluate it it will be exactly the same  but we've hidden 23 items in this list  and in this case I used it to represent  data but it doesn't have to be data I  could have used the  to represent code as well notebooks are  fully computable entities this is  something I always like to emphasize so  notebook elements are discoverable  objects which can be manipulated and I  won't be able to go very far in the time  and I have allotted but here's just a  couple of brief examples so in this case  I am assigning to the variable C an  object which represents this cell right  here and now I can operate on a cell and  what I'm going to do for this input is  I'm going to say take that cell and set  its background to be like blue and I can  operate in more significant ways on the  notebook in this case I'm going to use  notebook imports to do a sort of data  scraping operation on my notebook all of  these bulleted list items these use the  item style and so basically what I am  doing here is I'm taking all the cells  in the item style and returning them as  a list of strings so I'm going to  evaluate that and then you can see in my  result all of the bulleted points that  I've gone through and all the ones I've  yet to go through now it turns out that  all of the data in a notebook can be  represented as a Wolfram language  expression so here's an example where  I'm taking that C variable representing  the cell here and I'm just going to read  it directly and here we go and we can  see this is what the underlying  expression looks like and so I'm going  to make change to it I'm just going to  use standard Wolfram language operation  is to change it and this works very well  so in this case I'm just going to say  look for any case where it references  the variable C and let's replace it with  the variable the cell and then we're  just going to write the resulting so  Express  and back into our notebook and there we  go  an identical cell but except for having  replaced C with the cell what we've  covered so far is using notebooks  primarily as documents and we've seen  some examples of some embedded  interfaces but I wanted to make sure  that that was one of the things that's  that's very cool about notebooks is  documents is you can have live  interfaces with them and here in one  line of code with an embedded graphic so  that we can very easily see what's going  on here I've just created an interface  which simply calls color quantize with a  variable number of colors which is tied  to a slider and then it's going to show  the quantized version and it's going to  show the the histogram of the results  and let me and if I drag this across you  can see it more colors pop in and the  histogram then gets more complex as I  move to the right and this is something  that works in real time notebooks can be  used for other sorts of documents as  well the help system actually is fully  composed of notebooks and so if I open  the help system and let's go to a page  maybe the page for module for example  and this is a notebook just like any  other it has evaluatable cells and i can  run through and evaluate these things  myself and and I can make changes to  them if I want and everything just works  notebooks can also be dialog boxes and  palettes if you've used their product  before you've probably seen that we have  a number of interesting palettes here  and so for example here  the basic math assistant which you can  use to paste in various sorts of  typesetting what you may not be aware is  that many of our dialog boxes are also  notebooks and so for example the fine  dialog box is a notebook well it doesn't  look very impressive but because it's a  notebook I can I can type in typeset  expressions and then let's go searching  for a superscript x squared in our  documents and sure enough there we've  found it
 

